    "reconstructed_theorem_text": "INDEPENDENT MATHEMATICAL RECONSTRUCTION\n\nThis text reconstructs the anonymous displayed Lean proposition using only its approved definition context. It states no verdict about an unseen source and does not reconstruct a proof. Operators are real linear; their multiplication means composition, with the rightmost operator applied first. Equalities between L2 elements are equalities of almost-everywhere classes. In equations involving values or integrals, use any representatives as licensed by the displayed almost-everywhere claims.\n\n[00] Ambient data.\nLet E be a finite-dimensional real inner product space, equipped with its normed additive commutative group structure and a measurable space equal to its Borel measurable space. Let V : E -> R, let alpha and beta be nonnegative real numbers (Lean type R>=0), and let eta be a real number. Write alpha and beta for their real coercions in real formulas. The theorem has exactly the following six caller conditions.\n\n[01] Caller condition 1: alpha > 0.\n\n[02] Caller condition 2: alpha <= beta.\n\n[03] Caller condition 3: V is twice continuously differentiable on all of E, namely ContDiff R 2 V.\n\n[04] Caller condition 4: for every x,v in E, the second Frechet derivative satisfies both inequalities\n  alpha * ||v||^2 <= ((D(DV)(x))(v))(v),\n  ((D(DV)(x))(v))(v) <= beta * ||v||^2.\nBoth inequalities have the same globally quantified x and v; they bound the Hessian quadratic form, not an operator norm or a derivative at one distinguished point.\n\n[05] Caller condition 5: eta > 0.\n\n[06] Caller condition 6: beta * eta <= 1.\n\n[07] Boundary between premises and conclusions.\nThe ambient types/structures and the six conditions above are the inputs. All objects under later existential quantifiers, all identities involving them, their positivity/invertibility/isometry properties, and the centered-input constructions below are conclusions. Internal measurable-space and Hilbert-space instances introduce no additional caller conditions. In particular the proposition supplies a kernel, its operator realization, an invertible restricted positive root, and the subsequent polar and functional identities; the caller is not required to supply them.\n\n[08] Normalized reference measure.\nSet mu = volume.tilted(-V). Thus mu is the normalized exponential tilt of Lebesgue volume with weight exp(-V(x)); the notation is not an unnormalized density. The theorem will assert that this measure is a probability measure.\n\n[09] Joint Gaussian pushforward and its observed marginal.\nSet\n  J = (mu product stdGaussian(E)).map((x,z) |-> (x, x + sqrt(eta) z)),\n  nu = J.snd.\nEquivalently J is the joint law of (X,Y) obtained from independent X with law mu and standard Gaussian Z on E, with Y = X + sqrt(eta) Z. The measure nu is the law of the second coordinate Y. This is the actual joint measure defining all subsequent joint L2 spaces and conditional expectations.\n\n[10] Two distinct reflection maps.\nDefine the reflected-pair law and the transformation of the original joint space by\n  Lambda = J.map((x,y) |-> (y, 2x-y)),\n  F(x,y) = (x, 2x-y).\nThe first map has first coordinate y; F keeps first coordinate x. They must not be interchanged. Scalar 2 means real scalar multiplication in E.\n\n[11] Conditional measurable subspace.\nOn E product E use the product Borel measurable space. Let mY be the pullback of the measurable space on E by the second-coordinate projection, namely sigma(Y). Its inclusion in the product measurable space is supplied internally by measurability of that projection. Put\n  H = L2(J; R), K = L2(nu; R),\n  HP = lpMeas(R,R,mY,2,J),\nwhere HP is the closed Hilbert subspace of H consisting of sigma(Y)-measurable equivalence classes. Write iP : HP -> H for its inclusion.\n\n[12] Conditional expectation and pullback.\nLet EY : H -> HP denote the displayed condExpL2 for J and mY, and set P = iP composed with EY, a bounded real-linear endomorphism of H. The second projection is internally supplied as a measure-preserving map from J to nu. Its isometric linear pullback is\n  M : K -> H, M u = the class of ((x,y) |-> u(y)).\nThe measure-preserving record, the measurable-space inclusion, EY, P and M are internal definitions, not hypotheses to be supplied by the caller.\n\n[13] Probability and exact conditional-expectation range.\nThe conclusions include that mu, J and nu are probability measures, that HP is exactly the linear range of P, and that P(iP f) = iP f for every f in HP. The displayed equality P(f)=f uses the inclusion of HP into H on its right side.\n\n[14] A single every-state normalized kernel.\nThere exists one Markov kernel S : E -> Measure(E) such that, for every y in E, as an equality of measures,\n  S(y) = volume.tilted(x |-> -V((y+x)/2) - ||y-x||^2/(8 eta)).\nIts normalized weight is exp(-V((y+x)/2) - ||y-x||^2/(8 eta)). The argument of V is (y+x)/2 and the quadratic denominator is exactly 8 eta. This normalized formula is asserted for every y, not only nu-almost every y.\n\n[15] Conditional reflected law and both marginals.\nThe same S is a conditional kernel for Lambda: it gives the conditional distribution of the second coordinate of Lambda given its first coordinate. Equivalently Lambda is the measure obtained by taking its first marginal and then S. Moreover\n  Lambda.fst = nu, Lambda.snd = nu.\nThus the conditional first marginal is nu and the reflected law has the same measure nu as its second marginal. These are conclusions about the actual Lambda defined in [10].\n\n[16] Produced equivalence onto the conditional subspace.\nThere exists a real-linear isometric equivalence e : K -> HP with\n  iP(e u) = M u for every u in K.\nIn particular e has inverse e.symm : HP -> K; it is onto HP. For an endomorphism C of K, write e C e^(-1) for the induced bounded endomorphism of HP. This conjugation is the isometric star-algebra conjugation in the statement.\n\n[17] Produced joint reflection operator.\nThere exists a real-linear isometry U : H -> H such that for every g in H,\n  (U g)(x,y) = g(F(x,y)) for J-almost every (x,y).\nThe operator U is involutive, U(U g)=g for every g in H, and its underlying bounded operator is selfadjoint. All later occurrences use this same U. The almost-everywhere set in the representative equality may depend on g.\n\n[18] Produced observable Markov operator.\nThere exists a bounded operator T : K -> K which is selfadjoint and, for every u in K, satisfies all three properties\n  ||T u|| <= ||u||,\n  (T u)(y) = integral u(x) dS(y)(x) for nu-almost every y,\n  integral (T u)(y) dnu(y) = integral u(y) dnu(y).\nThe first is an L2 contraction bound, the second uses the very same normalized kernel S, and the third is preservation of the scalar integral. The representative formula is per observable, not a single claimed everywhere pointwise identity for all observables simultaneously.\n\n[19] Compression and off-diagonal operator.\nDefine bounded operators\n  A : HP -> HP, A = orthogonalProjectionOnto(HP) composed with U composed with iP,\n  B : HP -> H, B = (I_H - P) U P iP.\nHere the orthogonal projection in A has codomain HP. B has ambient joint space H as codomain, even though its values will lie in ker P. The factor P displayed in B is retained.\n\n[20] Observable compression conclusions.\nThe theorem asserts\n  A = e T e^(-1),\n  A is selfadjoint,\n  ||A f|| <= ||f|| for every f in HP.\nThe isometric equivalence in [16], not an arbitrary chosen coordinate change, is used here.\n\n[21] Off-diagonal range conclusion.\nFor every f in HP, P(B f) = 0. Thus B maps into the conditional-expectation kernel as a proved property, without redefining its ambient codomain H.\n\n[22] Positive root on the observable space.\nThere exists a bounded operator Gamma : K -> K such that\n  Gamma is positive,\n  Gamma Gamma = I_K - T T,\n  Gamma T = T Gamma.\nPositive means selfadjoint with nonnegative quadratic form. The square-root equation concerns I minus the operator square T T, not I minus T.\n\n[23] Transported root and the exact off-diagonal defect.\nSet GammaP = e Gamma e^(-1) : HP -> HP. Then\n  GammaP is positive,\n  GammaP GammaP = I_HP - A A,\n  GammaP A = A GammaP,\n  B.adjoint composed with B = GammaP GammaP.\nThe last identity is an equality of operators HP -> HP. In particular B.adjoint has domain all of H and codomain HP.\n\n[24] Produced constant and integral pairing.\nThere exists q in K with q(y)=1 for nu-almost every y, T q = q, and\n  inner_R(q,u) = integral u(y) dnu(y) for every u in K.\nThese statements refer to one common L2 element q. The pairing has q as its first displayed inner-product argument.\n\n[25] Centered conditional Hilbert subspace.\nSet qP = e q in HP and\n  HP0 = ker(f |-> inner_R(qP,f)) inside HP.\nThe normed-group, real-inner-product, and complete-space structures on HP0 are the inherited closed-kernel structures, all installed internally. Write i0 : HP0 -> HP for its inclusion. This is a subspace of HP, not a new L2 measure or a separately assumed domain.\n\n[26] Exact meaning of centering.\nFor every f in HP,\n  f belongs to HP0 if and only if integral (e^(-1) f)(y) dnu(y) = 0.\nThe mean is evaluated using the nu observable represented by e^(-1) f. The integral is not an integral of f against an unspecified measure.\n\n[27] Constant annihilation and the numeric lower-bound coefficient.\nThe conclusions include GammaP qP = 0. Define the real number\n  gamma = 2 sqrt(alpha eta)/(1 + alpha eta).\nThe theorem asserts gamma > 0. The numerator is exactly 2 sqrt(alpha eta); the denominator is exactly 1 + alpha eta. This gamma is defined from the original caller parameters.\n\n[28] Positive, uniformly positive, invertible centered restriction.\nThere exists a bounded GammaP0 : HP0 -> HP0 such that\n  i0(GammaP0 f) = GammaP(i0 f) for every f in HP0,\n  GammaP0 is positive,\n  GammaP0 - gamma I_HP0 is positive,\n  GammaP0 is a unit in the bounded-endomorphism algebra of HP0.\nThus the lower bound is on the exact centered restriction; in quadratic-form notation the third assertion is inner_R(f,GammaP0 f) >= gamma ||f||^2. These are produced properties rather than extra spectral assumptions.\n\n[29] One common bounded two-sided inverse.\nThere exists a bounded Inv : HP0 -> HP0 satisfying\n  Inv GammaP0 = I_HP0,\n  GammaP0 Inv = I_HP0,\n  ||Inv|| <= 1/gamma,\n  Inv is selfadjoint.\nThe norm is the operator norm. This same Inv is used in the polar factor and in the final functional. Neither a one-sided inverse nor an inverse of a different root suffices.\n\n[30] Centered restriction of the compression.\nThere exists a bounded A0 : HP0 -> HP0 with\n  i0(A0 u) = A(i0 u) for every u in HP0,\n  A0 is selfadjoint,\n  A0 GammaP0 = GammaP0 A0,\n  A0 A0 + GammaP0 GammaP0 = I_HP0,\n  A0 Inv = Inv A0.\nIn particular A0 is a restriction of the same A, not independently introduced observable dynamics.\n\n[31] Conditional complement.\nSet Hperp = ker P inside H and give it the inherited normed-group, real-inner-product, and closed-kernel complete-space structures. Write iPerp : Hperp -> H for its inclusion. Membership means P h = 0 in H. Hperp must not be replaced by the larger globally zero-integral subspace of H.\n\n[32] Intrinsic centered off-diagonal block.\nThere exists a bounded B0 : HP0 -> Hperp such that\n  iPerp(B0 f) = B(i0 f) for every f in HP0.\nIts adjoint B0.adjoint therefore has exact domain Hperp and codomain HP0.\n\n[33] Produced polar factorization.\nThere exists a bounded V0 : HP0 -> Hperp such that\n  V0 = B0 composed with Inv,\n  B0 = V0 composed with GammaP0.\nThe root and inverse are precisely those already produced in [28]-[29]. This is a factorization into the conditional complement, not merely a formal symbol with a separately assumed construction law.\n\n[34] Isometry into, without an onto assertion.\nThe same V0 satisfies\n  V0.adjoint composed with V0 = I_HP0,\n  ||V0 f|| = ||f|| for every f in HP0.\nThese assertions make V0 an isometric embedding into Hperp. There is no assertion that it is onto Hperp and no asserted identity V0 composed with V0.adjoint = I_Hperp.\n\n[35] Produced ambient residual projection.\nThere exists a bounded R : H -> Hperp such that\n  iPerp(R g) = g - P g for every g in H.\nThe codomain is ker P and the inclusion makes the displayed ambient subtraction precise. R is defined on all joint L2 observables.\n\n[36] Full-domain ambient adjoint formula.\nFor every g in H,\n  B.adjoint g = i0(B0.adjoint(R g)).\nBoth sides lie in HP. This relates the ambient adjoint with domain H to the intrinsic adjoint with domain Hperp through the produced R. It is not restricted to inputs already in Hperp or in the range of V0.\n\n[37] Residual compression of the same involution.\nDefine the bounded D : Hperp -> Hperp by\n  D = R composed with U composed with iPerp.\nFor every h in Hperp the theorem states the ambient representative identity\n  iPerp(D h) = U(iPerp h) - P(U(iPerp h)).\nThe occurrences of U and P are the same global operators already specified.\n\n[38] Whole-complement adjoint intertwining.\nAs bounded operators from all of Hperp to HP0,\n  V0.adjoint composed with D = -(A0 composed with V0.adjoint).\nThe domain is the entire conditional complement, not only the range of V0. The minus sign applies to the entire composed operator.\n\n[39] Quantification of centered inputs and their actual conditional means.\nFor every f in H satisfying integral f(z) dJ(z) = 0, there exists fP in HP0 with\n  i0(fP) = EY f in HP.\nOnly the scalar integral of the arbitrary joint input f is assumed in this final implication. Existence and centeredness of its conditional component fP are conclusions; they are not new caller assumptions.\n\n[40] Input residual, corrector and adjoint identities.\nFor that f define\n  fperp = R f in Hperp,\n  fV = V0.adjoint(fperp) in HP0.\nThen both identities hold:\n  B.adjoint(iPerp fperp) = i0(GammaP0 fV),\n  i0(GammaP0 fV) = GammaP(i0 fV).\nThese retain the distinction between the ambient adjoint, the intrinsic root and their inclusions. The corrector fV is actually computed from the joint residual.\n\n[41] Input norm decomposition and corrector bound.\nThe theorem asserts\n  ||f||^2 = ||fP||^2 + ||fperp||^2,\n  ||fV|| <= ||fperp||.\nThe first norm is in H, the second in HP0, the third in Hperp; inherited norms make the orthogonal decomposition meaningful.\n\n[42] Actual transformed joint input and its mean.\nDefine in H\n  g = U(P f - (f - P f)).\nThis is also U(2 P f - f), but the first expression is the displayed definition. The theorem concludes integral g(z) dJ(z) = 0. Zero output mean is not an additional input condition and g is not defined by formulas for its future components.\n\n[43] Output conditional mean and residual corrector.\nThere exists gP in HP0 with\n  i0(gP) = EY g in HP.\nDefine\n  gperp = R g in Hperp,\n  gV = V0.adjoint(gperp) in HP0.\nThus gP is the actual conditional-expectation component of the actual g, and gV is the actual adjoint image of its residual. The existence of gP is inside the centered-input conclusion.\n\n[44] Exact coupled component update.\nThose actual output components satisfy\n  gP = A0 fP - GammaP0 fV,\n  gV = GammaP0 fP + A0 fV.\nThe first root term has a minus sign; the second equation has a plus sign. Both equations are in HP0. They assert a relation between independently specified conditional/residual components; they do not define gP and gV by their right sides.\n\n[45] Pair-norm conservation.\nFor the same centered f and its constructed components,\n  ||gP||^2 + ||gV||^2 = ||fP||^2 + ||fV||^2.\nThis preserves the norm of the two-vector pair in HP0 product HP0; it does not replace fperp by fV in the input orthogonal decomposition [41].\n\n[46] Complete final functional identity.\nDefine the real function on HP0 product HP0 by\n  C(u,v) = (||u||^2 - ||v||^2)/2 - inner_R(A0(Inv u), v).\nThe same A0 and the same two-sided Inv from [29]-[30] occur here. For the actual input/output pair above, the final conclusion is exactly\n  C(gP,gV) - C(fP,fV) = -||fP||^2 + ||fV||^2.\nThe factor 1/2 applies only to the difference of squared norms in C; the inner-product term is subtracted with coefficient 1. The right-hand side has coefficient -1 on ||fP||^2 and +1 on ||fV||^2, with no omitted gamma or eta multiplier. This identity is concluded for every globally mean-zero f through the same component construction.\n\n[47] Common-witness scope.\nThe outer existential witnesses, in order, are S, e, U, T, Gamma, q, GammaP0, Inv, A0, B0, V0 and R. There is one common nested choice of these twelve objects satisfying all preceding and subsequent conjuncts simultaneously. The remaining two existential witnesses fP and gP are local to each globally centered f, with gP nested after the definition of that f's actual g. They are not extra global inputs, and the global operators do not depend on the particular f in [39].\n\n[48] Quantifier and almost-everywhere distinctions.\nThe Hessian bound is for every x and v. The kernel formula is for every state y as measure equality. Every operator identity, norm bound and inclusion equality has its explicitly displayed whole domain. Only the displayed representative identities for U, T and q are almost-everywhere assertions, respectively under J, nu and nu. The representative exceptional set can depend on the corresponding observable. The kernel's conditional-law assertion is a conditional-kernel property, not a demand for an everywhere uniquely specified conditional density beyond its explicitly asserted normalized formula.\n\n[49] Measures and exact centering domains.\nObservable means use nu on E; joint-input and transformed-output means use J on E product E. HP0 centers elements of HP via their e^(-1) observable in K. Hperp imposes zero conditional expectation under J. The ambient adjoint B.adjoint acts on H, while B0.adjoint and V0.adjoint act on Hperp. The inverse, A0, GammaP0, fP, fV, gP, gV and the arguments of C all belong to the HP0 setting.\n\n[50] Unresolved and verification boundary.\nUnresolved reconstruction slots: none. Every requested conclusion above is decoded from the anonymous complete statement and its approved definition context. The input's compiled flag is recorded as supplied metadata; this decoder ran no compiler and read no proof body. No source text, source identity, source anchor, review verdict or repair proposal was an input. This reconstruction makes no claim of source fidelity and supplies no extra assumptions to complete a theorem.\n",
